WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:06.596
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.596 --> 00:00:10.909
I have a very complicated
relationship with death.

00:00:10.909 --> 00:00:14.130
As a medical student
I've learned about death.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:19.616
I've been with patients as they've died,
and I can even talk about death.

00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:23.775
After all, I did title my talk,
Let's Talk About Death.

00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:27.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But in my heart, I don't accept death.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.350
I find it excruciating.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.860
Death just seems so unfair, so
out of my control, so final.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.120
Every since I was little, I've had this
horrible fear that I would die and

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.620
not have a chance to say good-bye.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.530
After my grandmother
passed away when I was 13,

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:50.480
I started writing a ton of notes to
everyone I knew to say good-bye.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.410
With the idea that maybe
somehow they would be sent out

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.605
in the event that I met an unexpected end.

00:00:56.605 --> 00:01:01.140
I pictured death by lightning.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.365
I was a pretty dramatic child.

00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:06.340
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I figured if I couldn't control

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.330
death, then at the very least, I could
try to make sure that my final actions

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.740
were according to what I valued, making
sure that the people I love feel loved.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.170
Now fast forward to my
training at the hospital.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:22.230
I'd like to tell you about a patient.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:23.790
Let's call him Sam.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:29.530
Sam was fantastic, he was 78 years old,
he had a huge family.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:33.810
And he loved telling me stories
about his old surfing days.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:38.210
Sam had cancer and
kidney disease and blood clots.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:39.940
He could no longer move on his own.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:41.840
When I met Sam,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.760
he had been brought in to the hospital
by an ambulance with internal bleeding.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.840
Sam was in a lot of pain.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:53.120
But even despite that, every time I would
walk into his room he would look at me and

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.218
say, hey baby, how you doin'?

00:01:56.218 --> 00:01:59.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Grandpa still had game.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:06.300
Unfortunately though,
his situation quickly became worse.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.970
As his medical team,
we decided to briach the topic of death.

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.670
We asked him, what would you want,

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:16.970
Sam, in case your heart stops beating,
or you stop breathing.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.240
Sam, would have nothing of it.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.540
Sam got angry, and his family got angry.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.020
And frankly,
I got pretty angry at the situation too.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.516
But, we had reached medicine's limits.

00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:34.098
Sam, ultimately went home, with no plan.

00:02:34.098 --> 00:02:36.952
He ended up coming back,
several times to the hospital,

00:02:36.952 --> 00:02:40.020
until he finally passed
away at the hospital.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.470
In a cold sterile environment
surrounded by machines.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.941
I've often wondered what would
Sam's final year have looked like,

00:02:48.941 --> 00:02:50.140
had he tried hospice?

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.010
Hospice is a service for the dying,
which optimizes for comfort and

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.120
symptom management rather than focusing
on trying to find one more cure.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.000
The research tells us that for
folks who try hospice,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.160
they often live longer and
more peaceful lives than those who don't.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.860
I don't know what Sam would have wanted.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:11.910
We never talked about death.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.680
But what I do know is that Sam
had never thought about death.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.480
And that's the case for most of us.

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.170
And what that means is, that when we
get to the point where we can more or

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.190
less anticipate death,

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:29.730
the time that we have left becomes
more about death and less about life.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.240
By show of hands, how many of
us are uncomfortable talking or

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:35.900
planning for death?

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:39.180
Me too.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.000
We don't think about death.

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:44.250
We don't talk about death.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:46.690
We don't plan for death.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.470
So I'd like to challenge us all
today to change our conversation,

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:54.410
to change our culture to include death.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:55.830
Because when we plan for

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:00.560
a meaningful death, not only do we
die better, but we also live better.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:02.680
So before we move on,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.810
I'd like to lay out a few basic facts
to put us all on the same page.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.150
Number one.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:10.928
Until Google comes up with a cure,
we're all going to die.

00:04:10.928 --> 00:04:14.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Number two.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.570
God forbid that somebody actually
gets struck by lightning, but

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.590
the majority of us will die of
terminal illness or old age.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:23.918
So let's focus on the latter two.

00:04:23.918 --> 00:04:28.840
And number three there's no wrong
choice when it comes to how you want

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:32.550
to spend the end of life but
often times it is a choice.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:38.190
Modern medicine has made
tremendous advances.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:41.210
But has also convinced us
that maybe we can cure death.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:43.170
We can't.

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.910
So instead, the conversation
becomes how do we want to die?

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:54.630
We spend our whole lives planning for
our milestones, writing our narratives,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:05:00.535
planning for that big job, or
the wedding, or even A LOWkeynotes talk.

00:05:00.535 --> 00:05:02.680
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.036
yet the final chapter
still sneaks up on us.

00:05:05.036 --> 00:05:08.700
In the US,
most of us don't die the way we want to.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.068
While the majority of elderly folks have
indicated that they'd like to die at home,

00:05:14.068 --> 00:05:17.123
only 25% actually do.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:21.800
In medicine, our default is to throw
a ton of interventions at you,

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.610
in an effort to prolong life.

00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:28.810
But that often comes at the cost of
quality time and spending your last time,

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:29.720
perhaps the way you want.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.860
The details of why we die
are different for everyone.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:38.224
But if we set our goals ahead of time,
then our doctors can help us figure

00:05:38.224 --> 00:05:40.877
out which interventions will
help us meet those goals.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.620
I'd like to tell you about
a community in Lacrosse, Wisconsin.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.290
It's a town in which they had
a huge widespread initiative

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:55.230
to get everyone to write
their advanced directives or

00:05:55.230 --> 00:06:00.030
the documents which you can specify
what you want at the end of life.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:01.210
They changed their culture.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.010
They changed their conversation.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.890
They even changed their health
care system to adjust to the need.

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.123
And the result?

00:06:07.123 --> 00:06:12.370
96% of people had an advance
directive on file when they died.

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:18.320
And most people wanted to die at home, and
that's how they died, on their own terms.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.920
For me the most important
reason why I care so

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.160
deeply about this is because of the impact
that it could have on my family.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:31.220
When we specify how we want to die,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:36.540
when we set our wishes forth we take the
burden of choice away from our families.

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.440
The research tells us that
those who have a plan in place,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.350
their families suffer less stress,
less anxiety, less depression.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.500
And they are free to grieve rather
then second guess their judgement.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.760
Planning for
death will never take away grief.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.090
Grief is inevitable.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:58.490
But second guessing is not.

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:03.580
So let's change our conversation.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:05.150
Let's think about death.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:06.160
Let's plan for death.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:08.210
Let's talk about death.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:11.730
Step one, thinking about death.

00:07:13.138 --> 00:07:15.760
In medicine we're talk
to ask several questions

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.650
when we start off the end
of life discussion.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.206
Question such as,
what matters most to you?

00:07:22.206 --> 00:07:24.630
What are your hopes?

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.800
What are your goals?

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.718
And what are you willing to trade-off for
them?

00:07:31.718 --> 00:07:36.000
You might recognize these questions
as the very same ones that we

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.994
use when we think about life.

00:07:37.994 --> 00:07:43.039
In fact, the career office has given us
a fantastic resource to help guide this.

00:07:45.045 --> 00:07:48.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Bet you'll never look at that

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:49.748
the same way again.

00:07:49.748 --> 00:07:54.316
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:54.316 --> 00:07:58.620
&gt;&gt; Step two, talk about death.

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:04.500
Sit down with your parents,
your grandparents, your friends, anybody.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:08.790
Ask them how do they want to die,
and tell them what you want.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:11.865
As the business school has taught us,
maybe role play it out first.

00:08:11.865 --> 00:08:18.390
And we won't know all of the details,
but if we paint the broad strokes early,

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:21.480
then we'll be able to fill in
those details better later.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.830
Again, there are few good resources
that can help guide this.

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.480
So, check out The Conversation Project,
Death with Dinner or

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.290
read Atul Gawande's book being Mortal.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:36.340
And finally, Step 3 three,
write your advanced directives.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:40.810
Change them over time and
tell your family, your friends, and

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:42.050
your doctors about this.

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:45.727
And even if you don't write an advanced
directive, at the very least,

00:08:45.727 --> 00:08:49.113
make sure that you designate a proxy and
everyone knows about it.

00:08:52.442 --> 00:08:57.051
Planning for death will never
take away the pain of loss,

00:08:57.051 --> 00:09:03.690
the pain of losing someone who is close to
us, and not having them with us anymore.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:08.820
I'm not sure that I'll
ever be okay with death.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:16.770
I'll never stop missing my grandma,
or Sam, or

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:20.020
the countless other people whose hands
I've had the privilege of holding.

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:26.310
But I believe that if we plan for

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:31.040
a meaningful death we can
die the way that we lived,

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:35.090
on our own terms,
because life is so precious.

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.531
So let's talk about death.

00:09:39.531 --> 00:09:42.714
Because in planning for
a meaningful death,

00:09:42.714 --> 00:09:45.991
we allow ourselves to
lead a meaningful life.

00:09:45.991 --> 00:09:46.978
Thank you.

00:09:46.978 --> 00:09:48.941
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:48.941 --> 00:09:53.599
[MUSIC]

